ule of  many different empires  under all of these rulers the armenian  people spoke an earlier form of armenian  but there was contact with other  languages and armenian underwent a  significant amount of influence from  them for instance it was influenced by  parthian a northwestern iranian language  and lingua franca of the parthian empire  then later by middle persian a  southwestern iranian language and lingua  franca of the sasanian empire that  succeeded the parthian empire a large  number of parthian and middle persian  words were adopted into classical  armenia which lasted from the 5th  century to the 11th century speakers of  persian and other indo-iranian languages  can still recognize a lot of words of  persian and parthian origin today for  example the word for thousand in  armenian  and in persian hazard the word for bad  in armenian vat and in persian bad in  both cases the armenian words were  borrowed from middle persian those are  just a couple of examples but there are  other words that are cognates with  persian ones that were not borrowed but  go all the way back to  proto-indo-european for example the word  for man in armenian mart and in persian  mert armenian may have also borrowed  from some extinct anatolian languages  such as hittite and louien as well as  semitic languages like arabic syriac and  assyrian and armenian may have a  caucasian substrate meaning that it  contains influence from a caucasian  language whose speakers adopted armenian  it was also influenced by turkic  languages under seldrick rule and  ottoman rule for example in its noun  agglutination the way multiple  grammatical suffixes are attached to  noun stems there has also been some  russian vocabulary borrowed into eastern  armenia  why do i need to specify eastern  armenian the historic land of armenia  was politically partitioned for much of  its history with western armenia and  eastern armenia coming under different  rulers the byzantine empire versus the  sassanid empire the ottoman empire  versus the safawi empire and the ottoman  empire versus the russian empire this  resulted in the development of two  spheres of the armenian language with  western and eastern dialects undergoing  different developments and with the  establishment of two standard varieties  western armenian based partly on the  constantinople dialect and eastern  armenian based partly on the yerevan  dialect but these standard varieties  differ from all spoken dialects of which  there are many eastern armenian is  official in armenia and is used in the  disputed territory of artsakh and by  armenian communities in russia georgia  and iran western armenian is used by the  remaining armenian community in turkey  istanbul in particular as well as in  much of the armenian diaspora throughout  the middle east and the rest of the  world the features and examples i talk  about in this video will be from eastern  armenian but first a message from our  sponsor me did you know that i have a  second channel on the geofocus channel  which i recently rebooted after a five  year hiatus i talk about various  countries of the world global issues and  so on if you like educational videos  like this one then check out the  geofocus channel the link is in the  description and pinned comment of this  video  and now back to features of armenian  for learners of armenian the language  seems to have a steep learning curve at  the beginning because of its challenging  script and sounds some armenian letters  can be easily confused with others as  they are distinguished only by a single  different stroke that might be hard to  see depending on the font also in  writing armenian has long strings of  consonants in a row with no vowels  between them they're not exactly  consonant clusters because in speech  schwa vowels are normally inserted which  separate the consonants but those vowels  are not written which makes these words  hard to read even for native speakers  when they en