ouns on monosyllabic nouns it's usually  nouns in armenian take a number of  suffixes in this order stem plural  suffix case ending definite suffix or  possessive suffix we already have the  first two  denounce them  the plural suffix  is the nominative case which has no case  ending but if it were a direct object it  would normally be in the dative case and  look like this  and there could be a possessive suffix  on the end too yes  meaning your children as a direct object  there used to be seven noun cases in  armenian but now there are five  nominative and dative which i just  mentioned ablative instrumental and  locative there's a difference in case  depending on whether a noun is human or  non-human when they're the direct object  human nouns take the dative case while  non-human nouns take the nominative case  i love my child  is in the dative case with a first  person singular possessive suffix i love  my job  is in the nominative case with a first  person singular possessive suffix in the  first and second person singular  possessive in other words for my and  your there are possessive suffixes that  can be used but there are also  standalone possessive adjectives that  can be used they come before the noun  which has a definite article suffix so  let's adjust those sentences we saw  before i love my child yes  i love my job  means my the definite article suffix is  um like this after a vowel and uh after  a consonant  there's no strict word order in armenia  as in these sentences the default word  order is sov subject object verb with  the auxiliary verb coming after the main  verb but the relationship between words  is indicated by cases and postpositions  so the word order can change so for  example if we take the first sentence it  could be yes  which is svo yes  which is svo with the auxiliary verb  before the main verb  which is osv this word order is less  commonly used but is possible  changes in order can emphasize different  parts of the sentence f