 not necessary yequel is the  perfect participle of the verb gal  meaning to come in most verb tenses the  main verb is not conjugated for the  subject the auxiliary verb is conjugated  and the main verb is a participle form  the verb phrase in this sentence has  past meaning but is the present perfect  form the present perfect is formed with  the perfective participle plus the  present tense of the auxiliary verb  i've started to eat or i started to eat  the past perfect is formed with the  perfective participle plus the imperfect  tense of the auxiliary verb  i had started to eat  the present tense is formed with the  present participle plus the present  tense of the auxiliary verb  i'm starting to eat the imperfect tense  is formed with the present participle  plus the imperfect tense of the  auxiliary verb  i was starting to eat the future tense  is formed with the future participle  plus the present tense of the auxiliary  verb  i will start to eat the future perfect  is formed with the future participle  plus the imperfect tense of the  auxiliary verb  i will have started to eat  the one tense in armenian that has its  own standalone conjugation without the  auxiliary verb is the ars tense or past  tense  i started to eat  there's a pattern to how we get the  different verb forms the infinitive ends  in el or al  as you can see in the infinitive for eat  the infinitive for start is  if you take away the l ending you get  the present stem the present stem plus  um gives us the present participle the  aerist or past stem is the same as the  present stem but for verbs ending in al  the suffix ats is added the era stem  plus l gives us the perfect participle  the infinitive plus the suffix u gives  us the future participle  where's the cafe  means where this little diacritic here  is the question mark used in armenian  it's placed over the last vowel in the  question word  here we see e again which means is  and  here it's in the nominative case with a  definite article suffix  the cafe is over there  is a demonstrative pronoun meaning over  there armenian has a three-way  distinction here i stare there meaning  away from the speaker but near the  listener  and over there meaning away from both  the speaker and the listener  i don't drink alcohol  word for word it's alcohol i don't drink  notice again that this sentence doesn't  have a personal pronoun because the  subject is obvious from the conjugation  of the auxiliary verb  is the present participle of  meaning to drink  and ch  is the first person singular negative  form of the copula verb or auxiliary  verb which means to be so it means am  not  notice that the negative form of the  auxiliary verb comes before the main  verb and not after it like affirmative  auxiliary verbs often do  is a negative prefix that can be added  to any form of this verb and also to the  ars tense or past tense form of verbs in  general  form of the verb  meaning to finish and it has the same  negative prefix  literally means homework  means home as an adjective  it's related to the noun for home or  house  this word has cognates in many  indo-european languages like latin domus  from which we get the english word dom  russian dom and others  means work this is the nominative form  with the first person singular  possessive suffix  earlier in the video i talked about  influences on armenian especially  ancient ones but it's interesting to  note that rather than the extensive use  of greek loanwords and roots that are  used in other indo-european languages  armenian often coins new words or calcs  using armenian roots for example this  word meaning telephone it was coined  using the armenian root hera meaning  tella or far plus the root jose meaning  to speak  then there's this word  meaning dialogue it was coined from yes  meaning to  plus  from jose again meaning to speak plus  a suffix that forms abstract nouns and  then there's this word  meaning logic it consists of the root  meaning firm or solid plus ban  meaning word or speech plus utsuyun the  abstract noun suffix again  armenian is not a very widely spoken  language but it's an important part of  the identity of armenian people around  the world and it's also fascinating as a  unique language isolate within the  indo-european language family and as a  language that's come into contact with a  wide variety of other languages both  influencing them and being influenced by  them  if you're a speaker of armenian how  similar is your native dialect to the  standard variety used in your community  and whether you use eastern armenian or  western armenian how well do you  understand the other variety  special thanks goes out to the langfocus  patreon supporters especially the ones  whose names appear right here on the  screen they are the top tier patreon  supporters so many extra special thanks  go out to them armenia lies on the edge  of the caucasus region and has come into  contact with various caucasian languages  check out this video to learn more about  the languages of the caucasus there are  parts of this video that will blow your  mind  you

